WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:20.250
When it comes to the world of the micro, there’s
a little bit of something for everyone.

00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.980
Do you feel a kinship with chill blobs that
contain hidden depths?

00:00:24.980 --> 00:00:28.000
Well, there’s plenty of amoeba to understand.

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.420
Do you love complex choreography?

00:00:30.420 --> 00:00:34.330
Well, then you might want to catch a performance
by bacillaria.

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:40.820
And if those don’t draw you in, there are
hunters, living jewels, and complicated lifestyles

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:42.900
aplenty to explore.

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:51.260
For microbiologist Dr. Lynn Margulis, the
draw was symbiosis.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:01:01.070
The microcosmos is made of many, many organisms
who don’t just occupy the same space, they collaborate.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:06.670
Whether it’s for protection, nutrition,
or just some help getting around, these interactions

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:17.509
may seem small, but their implications are
enormous for all life on earth.

00:01:17.509 --> 00:01:23.550
Through the second half of the 20th century,
Margulis’ dedication to understanding symbiosis

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:30.400
transformed our understanding not only of
microbes, but of ourselves.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:37.140
Her legacy is a complicated one, rooted in
a stubbornness that at times paid off in major

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:43.000
scientific understanding, but also made her
unwilling to acknowledge the limitations of

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.130
her work.

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:50.930
At the core of her efforts though is a dedication
to microbes and to understanding what they

00:01:50.930 --> 00:01:54.280
can teach us about the world.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:02:01.690
Lynn Margulis published many books and articles
about microbes for both academic and general

00:02:01.690 --> 00:02:03.060
audiences.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:09.690
But perhaps her most impactful work was also
one of her earliest, an article published

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.540
in 1967, shortly after she began working at
Boston University.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:23.519
The paper was titled “On the Origin of Mitosing
Cells,” and over its roughly 50 pages, Margulis

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:30.480
argued that some of the organelles inside
eukaryotes were actually once—long, long

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.260
ago—free-living prokaryotic organisms.

00:02:34.269 --> 00:02:41.400
What she was describing was the theory of
endosymbiosis, the idea that symbiosis between

00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:48.599
one ancient prokaryote inside another drove
the evolution of eukaryotes.

00:02:48.599 --> 00:02:54.760
We’ve discussed endosymbiosis in its own
episode, and you may have noted that it plays

00:02:54.760 --> 00:02:59.209
a recurring role in the lives of microbes
we’ve covered.

00:02:59.209 --> 00:03:05.069
This theory is now so well understood, and
it has so much explanatory power that, at

00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.950
this point it feels like a thing that we’ve
kinda always known.

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:16.859
But, indeed, the battle over the acceptance
of endosymbiotic theory was still going on

00:03:16.859 --> 00:03:19.040
during my lifetime.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:25.609
I remember being taught that this was probably
a thing, not that it definitely was.

00:03:25.609 --> 00:03:32.140
In her paper, Margulis also notes that she was
not the first scientist to consider the role

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.390
of symbiosis in evolution.

00:03:34.390 --> 00:03:39.740
The theory was first proposed by the Russian
botanist Konstantin Sergeevich Mereschkowski

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:45.840
in 1905, and later scientists had suggested
that the resemblance they observed between

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:53.099
certain eukaryotic organelles and certain
prokaryotic organisms was more than just coincidence.

00:03:53.099 --> 00:04:00.269
But while these bits and pieces of endosymbiotic
theory had been around before her, Margulis

00:04:00.269 --> 00:04:02.219
was the one who tied them together.

00:04:02.219 --> 00:04:09.329
“On the Origins of Mitosing Cells” focuses
on three examples of what Margulis thought

00:04:09.329 --> 00:04:17.820
were possible endosymbiotic events: the evolution
of mitochondria, chloroplasts, and flagella

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.549
from potentially prokaryotic origins.

00:04:20.549 --> 00:04:27.530
While the paper didn’t describe any experiments
of her own, she drew on the works of her predecessors

00:04:27.530 --> 00:04:31.729
to provide experimental evidence for her ideas.

00:04:31.729 --> 00:04:38.289
She also described predictions for future
experimental results that would support the theory.

00:04:38.289 --> 00:04:44.770
The result was a paper that didn’t just
present an argument, it laid out a multi-chapter

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:51.870
story of evolution that drew on Margulis’
understanding of biology and even atmospheric

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:59.930
chemistry to describe how symbiosis may have
driven eukaryotic evolution, and how scientists

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:03.340
might be able to verify that story.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:10.360
This paper, “On the Origin of Mitosing Cells”
was rejected more than a dozen times until

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:14.199
it was finally published in the Journal of
Theoretical Biology.

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:21.919
And her paper caused quite a stir, highlighted
and debated in both popular and academic formats.

00:05:21.919 --> 00:05:28.180
Some scientists were just as captivated by
the idea of endosymbiosis as Margulis was.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.449
Others dismissed her theory outright.

00:05:30.449 --> 00:05:37.090
In a paper called “The non symbiotic origin
of mitochondria,” the authors wrote that

00:05:37.090 --> 00:05:44.020
“while the symbiotic theory may be esthetically
pleasing, it is not compelling.”

00:05:46.330 --> 00:05:52.460
But while the conversation may have become
contentious, Margulis stuck to her theory.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:58.460
To her, the notion that these organelles could
have evolved solely through the accumulation

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:06.349
of thousands of mutations was both improbable
and unsupported by the evidence.

00:06:06.349 --> 00:06:12.250
Slow change was part of the evolutionary story,
but it was not all of it.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:18.800
And she thought symbiogenesis would explain
these more immense changes that were necessary

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.630
to the history of eukaryotes.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:28.470
And so Margulis continued to experiment with
her microbes and investigate the limits of

00:06:28.470 --> 00:06:29.510
her theory.

00:06:29.510 --> 00:06:32.020
But so did many others.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:39.310
In 1978, Robert M. Schwartz and Margaret O.
Dayhoff published “Origins of Prokaryotes

00:06:39.310 --> 00:06:47.349
, Eukaryotes, Mitochondria, and Chloroplasts,”
which was able to experimentally conclude

00:06:47.349 --> 00:06:54.860
the prokaryotic origins of chloroplasts and
everyone’s favorite powerhouse, the mitochondria.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:07:02.000
This work was among several that would confirm
that Margulis was right about those two organelles

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:03.780
in particular.

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:09.060
But as important in science as what you get
right is what you get wrong.

00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:14.680
No experimental evidence has turned up to
support Margulis’ theory that the flagella

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:20.789
that help many eukaryotic organisms move were
originally an organism of their own.

00:07:20.789 --> 00:07:24.030
She thought they may originally have been
spirochetes.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:29.659
But while flagellates and other aspects of
her theory have not panned out in experimental

00:07:29.659 --> 00:07:35.120
testing, Margulis would continue to defend
her ideas.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:39.879
Lynn Margulis eventually moved to the University
of Massachusetts Amherst, where she would

00:07:39.879 --> 00:07:43.220
stay until her death in November, 2011.

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:49.259
In an interview she gave to Discover Magazine
only a few months before she died, she said,

00:07:49.260 --> 00:07:52.810
“I don’t consider my ideas controversial.

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.520
I consider them right.”

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:59.760
It’s a statement that may seem admirable

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:03.720
when quoted against the history of endosymbiotic theory.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:10.050
But in that same interview, Margulis insisted
that AIDS is not caused by HIV, it’s caused

00:08:10.050 --> 00:08:16.930
by a syphilis-causing spirochete that has somehow
been rendered undetectable to all scientific

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:21.640
and medical scrutiny because of...symbiosis?

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:27.540
We’re not gonna litigate her statement here because
there’s no point in that: at the time she

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:33.289
gave the interview, there had been decades
of research connecting AIDS to HIV, as well

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:38.729
as decades of real-life suffering and death
caused by the virus.

00:08:38.729 --> 00:08:44.850
But this wasn’t the only time Margulis engaged
in what some might euphemize as “controversial

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:51.830
opinions,” but that at times took her fully
into conspiracy theories.

00:08:51.830 --> 00:08:59.160
Lynn Margulis’ work was part of a long history
of contrarianism that often appeals to us

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:05.840
because when they get things right, contrarians
push through established beliefs to reveal

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.630
hidden mechanisms of the world.

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:14.980
But when they get things wrong, they do so
at the expense of scientific rigor, intellectual

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:18.610
honesty, and just plain old decency.

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:26.550
And so it can sometimes be unclear how to
understand and process their work, unless--perhaps--we

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:33.460
take inspiration from Margulis’ fascination
with microbes and the communities they form.

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:40.250
Just as the light we shine through our microscope
informs the colors and structures that shine

00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:47.960
on the other side, our understanding of the microcosmos is shaped by the people who study it.

00:09:47.960 --> 00:09:54.040
Their experience, their personalities, their
biases—these all shape the questions they

00:09:54.040 --> 00:09:59.190
ask, the ways they ask them, and the conclusions
they draw.

00:09:59.190 --> 00:10:06.450
And so, science is at its strongest when,
like the symbiotic microcosmos that entranced

00:10:06.450 --> 00:10:15.150
Margulis, it is understood not as the product
of a single expert but as a collective expertise—the

00:10:15.150 --> 00:10:20.560
result of shared experiences that are constantly
in conversation with each other.

00:10:20.560 --> 00:10:27.610
That is what made “On the Origin of Mitosing
Cells” so powerful—the way Margulis so

00:10:27.610 --> 00:10:34.830
effectively synthesized her ideas with other
scientists’ work to describe a whole history

00:10:34.830 --> 00:10:43.060
of evolution that, while not without its flaws,
provoked a dialogue that altered our understanding

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:47.300
of biology and of the history of life on earth.

00:10:47.300 --> 00:10:53.950
And this impact makes the frustrations of
her legacy all the more acute.

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:59.770
Margulis would continue to draw on what she
learned from the world of microbes, co-authoring

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:06.890
a number of books with her son Dorion Sagan
that brought the wonders of microbial evolution

00:11:06.890 --> 00:11:07.880
to a wider audience.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:16.680
In one of their books titled Microcosmos,
they wrote: “It is tempting, even for scientists,

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:19.990
to get carried away by success stories.”

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:27.350
It’s a sentence that, against the backdrop
of both her scientific and unscientific efforts,

00:11:27.350 --> 00:11:31.920
reminds us that microbes can teach us a lot
about the world, but that there is also a

00:11:31.920 --> 00:11:34.320
limit to what we learn.

00:11:34.320 --> 00:11:40.500
And often, that limit is our own selves.

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:46.690
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:11:46.690 --> 00:11:51.110
Thank you as well to all of the people on
the screen right now.

00:11:51.110 --> 00:11:53.300
Those are our patrons on Patreon.

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:59.860
It’s a place you can go to support the content
you love, so that it will continue existing.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:03.950
So thanks to all of them for helping make
this continue to happen.

00:12:03.950 --> 00:12:06.100
What great folks.

00:12:06.100 --> 00:12:11.060
